## Why this entry, and why it stays unambiguous

- `{limit:number}` is a number slot, so `200` in E2E-001 is matched and any
  other deadline reuses the same entry. The unit stays the literal word
  `milliseconds`; a seconds variant, if a later Scenario needs one, is a
  separate entry with its own literal unit and so cannot overlap this one.
- `block: then` matches the step's block in E2E-001.
- No collision with the existing `the service answers "{reply}"` entry: that
  template requires a literal double-quoted run immediately after `answers`,
  while this one requires the literal word `within` there. No step text can
  satisfy both, so every step keeps matching exactly one template.
- Neither of the other two entries (`the mcp service is running`,
  `the {actor:role} sends "{message}"`) can match a step beginning
  `the service answers within`, so their Scenarios are unaffected.
- `terms: [mcp service, reply]` names only glossary terms that already
  exist, spelled exactly as the glossary spells them. `reply` is already
  defined as covering both the content and the delay, so the deadline
  meaning needs no new glossary entry.

